Loading 3D Models
For sprite-based games, every sprite draws with a single quad, which means it’s okay to
hard-code the vertex and index buffers. However, for a full 3D game, there are a lot of other
triangular meshes. For example, a first-person shooter has enemy meshes, weapon meshes,
character meshes, meshes for the environment, and so on. An artist creates these models in a
3D modeling program such as Blender or Autodesk Maya. The game then needs code to load
these models into vertex and index buffers.
